# Input
```ts
/* should generate diagnostics */
b.map(((x) => ({ ...x })) as MyCustomMapper);
b.map((x) => ({ ...x }) as Foo);
b.map((x) => ({ ...x }) satisfies Foo);
b!.map((x) => ({ ...x }));

```

# Diagnostics
```
invalid.ts:2:16 lint/nursery/noMapSpread ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

  ! This object spread copies an object for each item in the map() callback.
  
    1 │ /* should generate diagnostics */
  > 2 │ b.map(((x) => ({ ...x })) as MyCustomMapper);
      │                ^^^^^^^^
    3 │ b.map((x) => ({ ...x }) as Foo);
    4 │ b.map((x) => ({ ...x }) satisfies Foo);
  
  i The callback runs once for each item in the array.
  
    1 │ /* should generate diagnostics */
  > 2 │ b.map(((x) => ({ ...x })) as MyCustomMapper);
      │   ^^^
    3 │ b.map((x) => ({ ...x }) as Foo);
    4 │ b.map((x) => ({ ...x }) satisfies Foo);
  
  i A spread creates a new object and copies every property into it. Doing this for each item is slow and uses extra memory for large arrays.
  
  i If it's fine to change the original items, add the properties with Object.assign() or assign them directly. Keep the spread if other code needs the original items to stay unchanged.
  
  i This rule belongs to the nursery group, which means it is not yet stable and may change in the future. Visit https://biomejs.dev/linter/#nursery for more information.
  

```

